-- 32. EFFECTS, AND THE TAXONOMY IS NOT OURS TO INVENT. The owner asked whether
-- a standard one exists. It does, and in two versions that disagree usefully -
-- see f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice. The one to take
-- is Koka's basic set, because it is closed, named and ORDERED: `total`, `exn`,
-- `div`, `ndet`, `alloc`, `read`, `write`, `io`, with `pure = <div, exn>` and
-- `io = <st, div, exn, ndet>`. An order with a bottom and a top is the thing a
-- model can use; a flat list of labels is not.
--
-- IT IS BLOCKED ON THREE OF ITS OWN MEMBERS and that is why the dependency
-- rows are worth writing down: `exn` is item 28, `read`/`write` are item 30,
-- and `ndet` plus `io` are item 17 - which is to say the effect layer is not a
-- new question at all, it is the JOIN of four questions already queued.
-- DONE 2026-09-09, and the sentence above held literally: the layer IS the join
-- of four questions already queued, and it needed no machinery of its own for
-- three of them. `may_throw` became the `exn` projection of a general rule,
-- `plain_assign` and `member_node_v` became the read/write split with a heap,
-- and `resolves` carried every hidden call the destructuring and accessor work
-- had already found. What the layer ADDED is the ORDER — a fourteen-point
-- lattice with a derived bottom and top, a join and a meet, all four failure
-- modes of "this is not a lattice" empty — and the propagation that discharges
-- a label at a handler instead of a whole row.
--
-- THE ONE INDEPENDENT ORACLE, AND IT AGREES ROW FOR ROW. `eff_latent(F, exn,
-- none)` against `may_throw[code](F)`: `eff_exn_only[audit]` 0 and
-- `may_throw_only[audit]` 0 over 395 functions. Two different relations in two
-- different packs written a week apart, and the general machinery reproduces
-- the special-case one exactly — which is a cross-check on the discharge
-- condition, the `nearest_v` boundary and the call closure at once.
--
-- COST: peakRows 260 568 -> 264 181 on the corpus world, +1.39%, which takes it
-- from 52.1 to 52.8 per cent of the engine's 500 000-row space wall. A whole
-- fifth layer for one and a third per cent, because the join happens at the
-- FUNCTION BOUNDARY through `nearest_v` and not by a per-node walk over
-- `ast_within`. The wall time moved too and is not quoted: this machine was at
-- load average 30 with a dozen sibling worktrees running while it was measured,
-- and a timing taken under a neighbour is a timing about the neighbour.
--
-- 100 CELLS: 29 modelled, 52 waived, 1 irreducible (`debugger` is
-- `runtime_dependent`, not work), 18 open and every one of them owned by name
-- by the four items below. `sweeper` at this layer is 0 and `unqueued[audit]`
-- is 0.
work(w_effect_layer, "DONE 2026-09-09: layer(effect) over Koka's basic set, modelled as a LATTICE - eff_leq/eff_join/eff_meet with a derived bottom and top - plus a concrete column (surface, operation) whose names are derived and whose map into the lattice the surface packs owe").
work_state(w_effect_layer, done).
work_needs(w_effect_layer, w_exn_propagation).
work_needs(w_effect_layer, w_alias_store).
work_needs(w_effect_layer, w_env_api_surface).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_a_layer_declared_without_owners_is_not_a_state_the_queue_can_be_in).
work_spawned(w_effect_layer, f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 33-34 — WHAT THE CALL-GRAPH SWEEP FOUND, 2026-09-05. Eleven of its eighteen
-- cells were not residue, and they are two questions rather than eleven.

-- 33. THE CALL GRAPH STOPS AT THE MODULE BOUNDARY, and no gate could see it.
-- Measured: with BOTH files scanned into one store, `helper()` against
-- `import { helper } from './beta.mjs'` resolves to NOTHING — and so do the
-- default and namespace forms. The reason the acceptance is green anyway is
-- that the fixture corpus contains NO CROSS-FILE CALL AT ALL: alpha.mjs
-- imports only the tracer, beta.mjs is a second root, so the execution oracle
-- has nothing to be unsound about. A capability nobody has, in a corpus that
-- could not notice — the same shape as the leak audit that had no world.
-- The modules layer already resolves specifiers to files; nothing joins that
-- to `denotes`, which is the whole of this item.
-- PARTLY DONE 2026-09-07, and the second pass took THREE more forms that the
-- first pass had called uncheckable. Two of them needed one line of fixture
-- each — `import * as alphaNs` and `import adefault` in beta.mjs, both
-- pointing at a module that imports nothing from beta, so no cycle — and the
-- third needed alpha.mjs to grow a DEFAULT EXPORT WITH A CONSUMER, because
-- beta's `bdefault` is called by the harness and a form the harness calls is
-- not a form the model can be checked on.
--
-- THE LAST FORM COST TWO FILES AND NOT ONE. `export * from './x'` needs a
-- module that re-exports and a second module that imports FROM it, which is
-- gamma.mjs; that is enough to carry the names and NOT enough to check the
-- specifier, because beta.mjs imports alpha.mjs directly and the resolver had
-- already seen `./alpha.mjs`. delta.mjs is imported by nothing at all, so the
-- only declaration in the corpus that names it is gamma's second `export *` —
-- a fixture must be reachable ONLY through the construct it tests.
work(w_cg_module_boundary, "DONE 2026-09-07: r_imported_name and r_reexported_name; all seven import/export forms answered at both layers, with the last one needing a module reachable only through the re-export and a name that collides with the importing file's own").
work_state(w_cg_module_boundary, done).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_the_kernel_refused_exactly_and_nobody_read_the_refusal).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_the_boundary_had_no_site_to_cross).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_a_form_the_harness_calls_is_not_a_form_the_model_is_checked_on).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_a_probes_own_filter_is_the_third_way_it_lies).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_a_fixture_must_be_reachable_only_through_the_construct_it_tests).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_the_third_surviving_mutant_in_a_row_wanted_a_name_collision).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_sharing_a_relation_between_two_forms_moves_the_guard_to_every_reader).
work_spawned(w_cg_module_boundary, f_the_two_by_two_had_no_control_and_a_missing_axis_was_silent).
claim(queued, js, export_all_declaration,      none, callgraph, w_cg_module_boundary).
-- ...AND THE SAME SIX AT THE VALUE LAYER, added by the dataflow sweep on
-- 2026-09-05. It is ONE missing join and not two: `const h = helper; h()`
-- resolves to nothing for exactly the reason the call graph stops at the file,
-- and the item was named `w_cg_` only because the call-graph sweep found it
-- first. Measured with both files in one store, with a same-file control that
-- resolves normally two lines away.
claim(queued, js, export_all_declaration,      none, dataflow, w_cg_module_boundary).

-- 34. THE CALLS THAT HAVE NO CALL SITE. `call_kind` is the closed pair
-- {call_expression, optional_call_expression}, so every totality audit this
-- file owns quantifies over a set that three real calls are outside of:
-- ``tag`x` `` calls `tag` (measured: the node is in the tree, the call site
-- table has no row for it, and `unshaped[audit]` is 0 because it cannot see
-- past `call_site`); `for (x of it)` calls `it[Symbol.iterator]()` and
-- `.next()`; `@dec class` calls `dec`, and that one is additionally blocked by
-- the parser plugin being off. `await p` calling `p.then` is the fourth and is
-- the reason its kind cell stays open even though the CALLEE position
-- `(await f())()` resolves — one kind-level cell cannot hold two answers.
-- THE PRECEDENT IS IN THE RULE FILE ALREADY: `transfer_kind(new_expression)`
-- exists because "a model built only on CallExpression misses that edge with
-- nothing to point at". The argument was written and applied once.
-- ONE OF THE FOUR IS ANSWERED, 2026-09-07, and the INSTRUMENT decided which.
-- Asked of the runtime on a throwaway fixture before a rule was written:
--
--   tag`x`           V8 reports `useTag -> tag`   -- the enclosing function
--   for (x of it)    `useForOf -> [Symbol.iterator]` and `useForOf -> next`
--   await thenable   `<top> -> then`              -- the PROMISE MACHINERY
--
-- so the tagged template and the for-of are checkable and the AWAIT IS NOT: a
-- model deriving `useAwait -> then` would be contradicted by the oracle, not
-- confirmed by it, because the caller frame is outside the fixture entirely.
-- That is a property of the form rather than of the model, and it is why this
-- item now says three different things instead of one.
--
-- The tagged template closed at BOTH layers for one arm: a transfer site plus
-- the value layer's existing `resolves` carry. The for-of needs a custom
-- iterable the corpus does not have and whose method key is `Symbol.iterator`,
-- a computed member the scanner cannot reduce to a literal. The decorator
-- produces no node at all with the parser plugin off.
work(w_cg_invisible_calls, "DONE 2026-09-08: three of the four invisible calls have rules - r_tag_call, r_iterator_protocol, r_decorator_call - and the fourth is not one of them: an await's `.then` is a standard-library method with no node, re-pointed at w_env_api_surface with the measurement written down").
work_state(w_cg_invisible_calls, done).
work_spawned(w_cg_invisible_calls, f_the_instrument_decides_which_invisible_call_can_be_closed).
work_spawned(w_cg_invisible_calls, f_a_guard_over_a_runnable_corpus_has_no_site_to_bite_on).
work_spawned(w_cg_invisible_calls, f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_smuggled_in_two_kinds).
work_spawned(w_cg_invisible_calls, f_the_for_of_was_blocked_by_naming_and_not_by_the_scanner).
work_spawned(w_cg_invisible_calls, f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours).
-- ...AND THE FOR-OF LANDED, 2026-09-07, as `r_iterator_protocol`. The design
-- fork is worth more than the rule: a for-of is ONE node and TWO calls, and
-- `ambiguous_call[audit]` reads a site with two answers as an
-- over-approximation that must be visible. That reading is right for a callee
-- POSITION and wrong here, because both calls happen — so only the
-- `[Symbol.iterator]` hop goes through `resolves` and the `next` hop goes
-- straight into `calls`. Measured both ways: through `resolves` it takes
-- `ambiguous_call` from 8 to 9.
-- AND THE FOR-OF NOTE ABOVE WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, measured 2026-09-07:
-- the scanner sees the WHOLE protocol key. `{ [Symbol.iterator]() {} }` comes
-- out as an `object_method` with `computed=true` whose `key` child is a
-- `member_expression` over two named identifiers, `Symbol` and `iterator`. A
-- rule does not need the key reduced to a literal, it needs the SHAPE, and the
-- shape is all there. What actually blocks the for-of is NAMING, on both sides
-- of the comparison at once: the model has no `fn_name` for a computed method,
-- so an edge to it carries no callee name, and the oracle's `frameName` takes
-- the last dot-segment of `[Symbol.iterator]` and reports `iterator]`. Twelfth
-- note in this loop refuted by the first probe, and the first refuted by a
-- probe run against a note written the same day.
-- The tagged template's two claims are GONE rather than kept beside a done
-- note, and the difference matters: `queue_stale[audit]` says a claim on a shut
-- cell under an OPEN item is a lie, and it said so within one run. A claim
-- survives a closed cell only when the ITEM is done, which is what
-- `false_done[audit]` then guards.
-- ...AND THE FOR-OF'S CLAIM IS GONE THE SAME WAY THE TAGGED TEMPLATE'S WENT,
-- 2026-09-07: `queue_stale[audit]` reads a claim on a shut cell under an open
-- item as a lie and says so within one run, so closing the cell and retiring
-- the claim are one edit rather than two.
-- ...AND THE AWAIT IS NOT ONE OF THESE. It was claimed here because it looked
-- like the others — a call the grammar does not show — and MEASURING the corpus
-- rather than the grammar says it is a different question, so the claim moves to
-- `w_env_api_surface` by name rather than sitting under an item that cannot
-- finish it. What was measured, 2026-09-08, over the whole corpus:
--
--   * FOUR `await_expression` nodes. Two have a value for their argument
--     (`mkAlef()` reaches the `alef` declaration, `mkHolder()` an object
--     literal); `await unsettled` and the one in `main`'s array reach nothing.
--   * `member_value[flow](O, "then", V)` is EMPTY, and that is a fact about the
--     corpus rather than about the query: `member_value[flow](O, "next", V)`
--     returns two rows and the relation carries 140 distinct keys, none of them
--     `then`. So there is no user-written thenable anywhere and the `.then`
--     every one of these four awaits would call is `Promise.prototype.then`.
--   * `builtin_prototype` names six prototypes and `promise` is not among them,
--     and `stdlib_member[audit]` — the relation that exists to collect exactly
--     this residue — names six sites, none of them an await.
--
-- So the callee is a standard-library method with NO NODE IN THE PROGRAM, which
-- is `w_env_api_surface`'s question and the same one `w_prototype_of_a_value`
-- already declares it needs. The oracle limit recorded in alpha.mjs stands
-- beside it and is not the same claim: V8 attributes a thenable's `.then` to
-- `<top>` because the promise machinery makes the call, so even a corpus that
-- HAD a user thenable would be modelling something the instrument contradicts.
-- Two independent reasons, and only the first is what the queue can act on.
-- ...AND EVERY BULLET ABOVE IS TRUE AND THE CONCLUSION IS NOT, measured
-- 2026-09-08. The corpus measurements re-run and agree (`member_value[flow](O,
-- "then", V)` is 0 with `"next"` at 2 over 162 distinct keys now, and all four
-- awaits take a native promise). What is false is the sentence they support:
-- "the `.then` every one of these four awaits would call is
-- `Promise.prototype.then`".
--
--   * PROBE, with `Promise.prototype.then` monkey-patched and a POSITIVE
--     CONTROL that the patch is live (`p.then(f)` counts 1): `await <native
--     promise>` calls it ZERO times, `await 5` zero, on V8 11.3 (node
--     20.20.0) and V8 13.6 (node 24.13.0) alike. The specification's `Await` uses
--     PerformPromiseThen, an INTERNAL operation. There is no library method in
--     this construct, so the cell never belonged to `w_env_api_surface` and the
--     dependency it was re-pointed at could never have discharged it.
--   * A USER thenable's own `then` IS called, exactly once — so an await's
--     callee, when it has one, is a function this program may well contain.
--   * AND THE ORACLE DOES NOT CONTRADICT IT, IT CANNOT SEE IT. The `.then` runs
--     on a stack of DEPTH ONE: `st[1]` is undefined, so trace.mjs writes
--     `file: ""`, and `runOracle` keeps a frame only when the CALLER's file is a
--     run file. Measured on a live thenable in a copied fixture directory: the
--     instrument records `<top> -> settle file="" line=0` and the harness keeps
--     NOTHING. `<top>` in that file is what an ABSENT caller renders as and also
--     what a module-top-level caller renders as — two facts under one name, and
--     that collision is what the note above read as a contradiction.
--
-- SO THE CELL IS ANSWERED, and the answer is the question the item asked. This
-- layer's caller is a LEXICAL REGION — `nearest_fn` or the module root — and an
-- await's call has neither. `performed_call[code]` in rules/js-callgraph.rofl
-- names the callee and refuses the caller column; `await_no_call[code]` says an
-- await performs no call at all (2 of 5 sites); `await_value_unknown[audit]` is
-- the residue (2 of 5), and it is deliberately NOT wired into `frontier_at`,
-- because that relation is keyed by the caller and the whole finding is that
-- this class of call has none.
-- retired 2026-09-08: the cell is answered — `handled(js, await_expression,
-- callgraph, r_await_performs_then)`.
-- `decorator` LEFT THIS ITEM ON 2026-09-08, closed by `r_decorator_call` while
-- the plugin work was going on. It was correctly claimed here — a decorator IS
-- an invisible call, which is what this item is about — and the claim is
-- retired rather than left standing, because `queue_stale[audit]` calls a
-- claim on an answered cell a lie. It reported this one within a run.
-- ...and their value halves: what a decorator or a tagged template EVALUATES
-- to is what the call returns, and the call has no site to hang a return on.
-- ...AND ITS DATAFLOW CLAIM WENT WITH IT, to w_decorator_replaces_its_target.
-- `double_owned[audit]` reported the overlap the moment the second item was
-- written, and the split is not bookkeeping: what is left at this layer is
-- that a decorator RETURNS A REPLACEMENT, which is a value question, and this
-- item is about calls the grammar does not show.

-- ...and the two the sweep handed to items that already existed. `this()` is a
-- question about what `this` denotes, which is scope; `(yield v)()` is the
-- consumer's value arriving in the middle of a suspended body, which is the
-- generator protocol's other direction.
claim(queued, js, this_expression,  none, callgraph, w_scope_binding).

-- 35 — THE CONTRACT UNDER ALL THREE BLOCKED CELLS. `cell_blocked` says a cell
-- waits on the scanner and names no item, so two items sit in
-- `nothing_workable[audit]` with their blocker written only in prose. It is
-- one sentence: scanners/js_ast.ts emits SCALAR own properties, and
-- `TemplateElement.value` is `{raw, cooked}`, a nested object — so ``o[`k`]()``
-- is fixed at parse time and not derivable from any fact we have. Entered
-- because the call-graph sweep left `template_literal x callgraph` as the one
-- cell nobody owned, and the honest owner is the contract rather than a bucket.
-- DONE 2026-09-08. The contract now flattens a nested object whose members are
-- all scalars, which is one general rule rather than a case for templates, and
-- the measurement that justified it is in the retired blocker note above: the
-- exclusion was one property wide. Both cells closed; the claims go with them,
-- because `queue_stale[audit]` calls a claim on a shut cell under an open item
-- a lie and `false_done[audit]` guards the other direction.
work(w_scanner_nested_values, "the scanner emits scalar own properties only, so a template's text never becomes a fact").
work_state(w_scanner_nested_values, done).
work_spawned(w_scanner_nested_values, f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language).

-- 36 — THE JOIN PLANNER. A body is evaluated left to right, so the cost of a
-- rule is a property of the order a human wrote it in. Measured 2026-09-05:
-- one badly-ordered premise was 39% of the whole fixpoint, and reordering nine
-- bodies by hand took it from 24.7 s to 4.9 s with byte-identical answers.
-- The hand fix does not generalise - the next rule has the same trap - and the
-- gate that now watches for it (test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts) reports the
-- offender rather than preventing it. THE REMEDY IS A PLANNER that orders
-- literals by boundness at match time, and it carries the risk the other half
-- of this defect already names: under negation, order changes the ANSWER, so a
-- planner may not reorder across a negative literal. Same item, both hats.
-- DONE 2026-09-09, AND THE ITEM'S OWN QUESTION WAS ANSWERED BY REFUSING HALF OF
-- IT. The planner it asks for - order literals by boundness - was built first in
-- its strongest form: greedy join ordering, exact minimisation of the sum of
-- intermediate result sizes by subset DP over every legal order, fed PERFECT
-- statistics off a finished 434 149-fact store. Scored against the five hand
-- reorders of 2026-09-05 and 2026-09-09 it got FIVE OF FIVE: `has_return`,
-- `encloses_s` and `param_hidden` reordered exactly as the humans did,
-- `tdz_deferred` rated at 1.01x (the `worth nothing` that was measured), and
-- `closer_v` KEPT as written with its +65% variant reverted. A boundness
-- heuristic gets two of those wrong, so the model was doing real work.
-- AND ALL FIVE WERE RETRODICTIONS. It proposed two orders nobody had measured
-- and both were built: -0.32% against a predicted 19.3x, and +326% - the world
-- 3 521 279 rows to 15 015 237 - against a predicted 4.43x IMPROVEMENT. The
-- mechanism is measured: the estimate for `ident[code](U, Name)` with Name bound
-- is the mean rows per name, 4.41 over 553 names, and the join asks it about
-- PARAMETER names where the same quantity is 648, because one parameter is
-- called `n` and `n` occurs 758 times against a median of 2. A join's probe
-- values are correlated with the tail of the distribution it probes.
--
-- WHAT SHIPPED IS THE HALF THAT NEEDS NO STATISTICS. Asking what the three real
-- repairs had that the two non-repairs did not gives a criterion with no numbers
-- in it: a positive literal sharing NO VARIABLE with anything before it is a
-- CROSS PRODUCT where it stands. `planBody` holds such a literal until something
-- binds it, and a negation or a builtin drains the hold ahead of itself, so a
-- positive can never overtake a `not` or an `is` - which is the risk this item
-- names in its own sentence. Control-flow world 3 521 279 -> 3 353 932 rows
-- (-4.75%) with all 199 959 program facts, 177 218 firings and 434 149 facts
-- IDENTICAL, and the whole non-JS suite at 956/963 with the same seven
-- pre-existing failures. The item's point is the other numbers: with it in, the
-- PRE-REPAIR spellings of `encloses_s` and `param_hidden` cost 0.00% more than
-- the repaired ones and the `tdz_deferred` swap costs 0.00%, where they were
-- +37.27%, +119.87% and -0.04% - three bodies whose written order stopped being
-- a cost decision. `has_return` is the honest partial, +66.62% -> +1.52%.
work(w_join_planner, "DONE 2026-09-09: a cross-product literal is held until something binds it - the half of join order that needs no statistics, because the half that needs them was measured wrong twice on its own proposals").
work_state(w_join_planner, done).
work_needs(w_join_planner, w_body_order_is_load_bearing).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_the_engine_has_no_join_planner).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done).
work_spawned(w_join_planner, f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct).

-- 37 — THE MODULE GRAPH HAS ONE EDGE KIND, and the sweep of the last bucket is
-- what put it in one sentence. rules/js-modules.rofl models the import
-- declaration and the dynamic import, and NOTHING ELSE about how modules
-- connect to each other. Three faces, measured on one probe:
--   `export { q } from './y'` and `export * from './z'` produce ZERO import
--     sites while three ordinary imports in the same file produce three. Both
--     DEPEND and FLOW by the file's own definition. Their cells had been
--     WAIVED with a reason whose own comment called the work undone.
--   `require('./x')` parses as an ordinary call under `sourceType: 'module'`,
--     so the model can see it and says nothing. Whether CommonJS is in scope
--     is the owner's call, and `bare` beside it already carries his
--     `out_of_scope` - so this one is FLAGGED rather than assumed.
--   `import x from 'pkg/sub'` carries `not_yet` on the subpath shape: the
--     specifier resolves through a package's `exports` map, which is the same
--     on-disk question as `bare`. ALSO THE OWNER'S to scope, and named here so
--     it is not silently done or silently dropped.
-- TWO OF THE FIVE CELLS CLOSED 2026-09-08, and the item stays open because the
-- other three are the OWNER'S to scope and were flagged as such above.
--
-- RE-MEASURED FIRST, on the tree this layer is tested against and not on the
-- note: `import_site` 17, `depends` 10, and exp.ts — which carries
-- `export * as everything from './b.ts'` and `export * from './types2.ts'` —
-- appearing in NEITHER. The two declarations' `source` children were already in
-- the store. After: `module_site` 24, `depends` 16, six targets out of exp.ts.
--
-- IT REALLY WAS A JOIN AND NOT A MECHANISM. `depends` needed nothing at all —
-- it reads `resolved_import` and `site_file`, and both follow `module_site`
-- once the union exists — so making the site was the whole of the edge.
-- `reexport_offers` is the other half the brief predicted: the resolver says
-- which FILE, section 4b says which NAME, and putting them together is one
-- rule. What did need rules of its own is FLOWS and EVALUATES, because the
-- erasure markers sit on different nodes for an export than for an import and
-- one of the four forms has no specifier to carry one.
--
-- THE GATE THAT COULD NOT SEE ANY OF IT, and this is the part worth keeping:
-- the strongest assertion in test/js-modules.test.ts is that the oracle and the
-- model enumerate the SAME NUMBER of sites — and both enumerations were blind
-- to the same node types, so it was green over a set that excluded seven real
-- places. Recorded as f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing.
-- THE SECOND HALF, 2026-09-08, and it closed the third of the three cells and
-- SETTLED THE OPEN FINDING. What is left is two cells whose reasons are now
-- measured instead of inherited, and both of them are the owner's to scope.
--
-- THE DEFAULT FORM CLOSED ON THE NAMING HALF, exactly where the note above
-- predicted, and the measurement widened first: scanned over EIGHT spellings
-- rather than the one, they split FOUR AND FOUR on whether an internal name
-- exists at all, so the column is PARTIAL by the grammar and
-- `default_anonymous` is its written-down complement.
-- AND THE FOUR THAT DO NAME SOMETHING NEED TWO ARMS, which one probe would not
-- have shown: `export default function named() {}` carries the name on the
-- declaration's `id` CHILD, and `export default q` IS the name — babel gives an
-- Identifier a `name` attribute and no `id` child at all. A rule reading `id`
-- alone reports the second form ANONYMOUS, which is the safe-looking direction
-- and is wrong about a name that really is in this module. Three fixture files
-- with one default export each, because the grammar allows one per module and
-- the three arms cannot otherwise be separated.
-- `default_external` is always `"default"`, the string section 4 already writes
-- for an ImportDefaultSpecifier's `imported` name, so the two ends of the pair
-- meet on ONE spelling; `default_unaccounted[audit]` says the pair is total.
--
-- AND IT GOT AN EXECUTION ORACLE, which the resolver could not be. A default
-- export has no specifier to resolve, so the question is not WHICH FILE but
-- WHICH NAME THIS MODULE OFFERS — and node answers that by IMPORTING the
-- module: `o_node_module_namespace` reads `Object.keys(ns)` and
-- `ns.default.name` over test/fixtures/js-call, which RUNS, and agrees set for
-- set with `default_internal`. It is checked in BOTH directions, because a set
-- comparison cannot see a module that offers no default being given one.
--
-- THE OPEN FINDING IS SETTLED AND THE GUARD IS NOT TRANSPLANTABLE. The
-- dataflow layer's `not reexport_decl` cannot be copied into section 4b, and
-- the reason is the GRAMMAR: measured against babel 8, `export * as ns;` is a
-- SYNTAX ERROR, so every ExportNamespaceSpecifier sits under a declaration
-- with a `source` and the narrowing would empty that kind's landed cell in
-- EVERY program rather than deleting one row here. The row is kept and the
-- silence is closed instead — section 5c's `export_internal_in` says which
-- module an internal name lives in, in one rule with two arms, and
-- `export_internal_elsewhere` names the four rows the finding was about, two
-- of which are type-only and therefore outside `reexport_offers`.
--
-- THE TWO CELLS THAT STAY, with the reason each actually has:
--   `call_expression` — RE-MEASURED AND THERE IS NO SITE. 265 files parsed,
--     2 unparseable, walked for a CallExpression whose callee is `require`:
--     ZERO in the tree and in both scanned corpora. And on a probe holding a
--     real one, `module_site` 24 -> 24 with `unaccounted_site`,
--     `site_without_kind`, `reason_missing` and `resolve_gap` all 0 -> 0: this
--     layer's whole frontier is keyed on `module_site`, so a module construct
--     outside it moves no row of it. That is
--     f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing one level up.
--   `import_declaration x subpath` — THE RECORDED REASON WAS WRONG TWICE. The
--     note above called it the same on-disk question as `bare` and named a
--     package's `exports` map. Put to node's own resolver: `#sub` resolves to
--     sub/c.ts INSIDE the scanned tree and `@babel/parser` resolves into
--     node_modules OUTSIDE it. It is the IMPORTING package's own `imports`
--     map, in a file the fs emitter already walks. What it needs is that
--     file's CONTENT — a third blind host loan beside the string algebra and
--     the file tree — plus a walk that can start at the package root. Real
--     work with a shape, not a resemblance to `bare`.
-- NEITHER IS GIVEN A VERDICT HERE. `out_of_scope` is the owner's word and the
-- only ours-reason that takes a cell out of the queue; both stay `not_yet`
-- with the measurement beside them, and both measurements are assertions in
-- test/js-modules.test.ts rather than prose.
work(w_mod_beyond_the_import, "PARTLY DONE 2026-09-08: the re-export edge, then the default export's naming half against an execution oracle; `require` has no site in the whole tree and the subpath specifier needs a third host loan, and both stay the owner's to scope").
work_state(w_mod_beyond_the_import, open).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_the_default_export_has_an_erasure_marker_that_lies).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not).
work_spawned(w_mod_beyond_the_import, f_a_require_site_moves_no_row_of_the_frontier).
-- THE NOTE'S ABSENCE CLAIM, AS A QUERY, 2026-09-09 (w_note_is_not_evidence).
-- `require has no site in the whole tree` was measured by a PURPOSE-BUILT WALK
-- over 265 files, which is a world nobody else builds and nobody can re-run: it
-- is exactly the shape CLAUDE.md forbids writing a witness from. What CAN be
-- stated is the same claim in a world that is declared, cheaper, and NARROWER -
-- the five call fixtures - and the honest thing is that the witness says less
-- than the prose. The prose keeps the 265 files; the row keeps the part anyone
-- can run, and goes red the day a fixture gains a `require`.
--
-- IT IS AN OVER-APPROXIMATION ON PURPOSE. `query` takes one literal, so
-- `a call whose callee is the identifier require` cannot be joined; ANY
-- identifier spelt `require` is asked instead. For an ABSENCE that is sound -
-- zero over a superset is zero over the subset - and it would not be for a
-- presence, which is the asymmetry worth carrying.
--
-- THE CONTROL IS THE OTHER HALF AND IT IS NOT DECORATION.
-- `ast_name[code](N, require)` - the same literal with the constant written as
-- an ATOM instead of a string - also answers 0, with no error and nothing
-- `unpopulatable` can see, because names are strings in this relation and kinds
-- are atoms in `ast_node`. A control returning rows is the only thing that
-- separates a measured absence from a mis-quoted constant. `twin` is the
-- control because test/js-controlflow-scope.test.ts pins it in eight
-- assertions, so it cannot die quietly.
witness_absent(w_mod_beyond_the_import, w_js_corpus,
               "ast_name[code](N, \"require\")", "ast_name[code](N, \"twin\")").
-- ...and the two claims go with their cells, because `queue_stale[audit]` calls
-- a claim on a shut cell under an open item a lie and said so within one run.
-- The default form's claim STAYS, and its reason is measured rather than
-- inherited: scanned beside the six re-export forms, `export default function
-- named() {}` emits no `source` child of any kind, so it is not an edge — what
-- is left of that cell is the naming half, which is section 4b's question and
-- the one export form with no specifier node to hang it on.
-- ...AND IT GOES THE SAME WAY 2026-09-08, for the same reason the other two
-- did: section 4c answers the naming half, so a claim on a shut cell would be
-- the lie `queue_stale[audit]` reports within one run. The two that remain are
-- the ones with no verdict this item may write.
claim(queued, js, call_expression,          none,    modules, w_mod_beyond_the_import).
claim(queued, js, import_declaration,       subpath, modules, w_mod_beyond_the_import).

-- 38 — THE VOCABULARY'S OWN HOME. `facts/js-kinds.rofl` is named for the kind
-- vocabulary and holds a minority of it; the rest is spread over four layer
-- packs, each having declared what it needed first. Two sweeps in two days
-- wrote verdicts about kinds their own pack had not declared, and
-- `orphan_claim[audit]` reported 3 and then 31. Both were closed by
-- re-declaring - which is correct, and is also how a 59-kind vocabulary comes
-- to be written out four times. The choice is the owner's: move every
-- `node_kind` to the pack whose name promises them and load it everywhere, or
-- keep `a file that makes a claim declares the kind` and accept the copies.
-- ...AND THE CHOICE WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN MADE, 2026-09-09. It is seven
-- packs now, not five, and 32 of 100 kinds. NOT ONE ROW MOVED, because the
-- measurement refutes the branch that would have moved them: executing the
-- proposal on a copy of the text and rebuilding the six distinct fact-pack
-- worlds this suite builds, the mechanism fixture goes 32 -> 100 kinds, the
-- call-graph fixpoint 84 -> 100, the shape axis 86 -> 100, the module boundary
-- 86 -> 100 and the era scale 86 -> 100. EVERY PARTIAL WORLD BECOMES THE FULL
-- ONE. The copies are not waste, they are what the partial worlds cost.
-- WHAT THE ITEM ACTUALLY LACKED WAS THE RULE, which was a comment in three
-- files: a `node_kind(js, K)` row belongs in the pack of layer P iff P SPEAKS
-- ABOUT K - some fact P asserts carries K in a position that holds kinds - or P
-- is the BASE pack every world loads. rules/js-pack-home.rofl derives that once
-- from the reified facts rather than deciding it per row, discovering the
-- thirty-two kind positions instead of listing them; `kind_home_unbacked[audit]`
-- is EMPTY over all seven packs and 24 rows fire the moment the base clause
-- goes. THE HALF THAT IS STILL THE OWNER'S is whether to gather anyway and pay
-- for it, and that stays open in the finding rather than being decided here.
work(w_vocabulary_home, "DONE 2026-09-09: the rows are already where the rule says they belong - measured, zero violations over seven packs - and the gather the item's name suggests would flatten all six partial worlds into the full one; the rule is stated and enforced in rules/js-pack-home.rofl").
work_state(w_vocabulary_home, done).
work_spawned(w_vocabulary_home, f_the_kind_vocabulary_is_scattered_across_the_packs_that_needed_it).
work_spawned(w_vocabulary_home, f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home).
work_spawned(w_vocabulary_home, f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does).
work_spawned(w_vocabulary_home, f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one).

-- 39 — RANGE RESTRICTION FOR NEGATION, entered 2026-09-05 by the work that
-- fixed the ORDER half beside it. A variable appearing only inside `not p(...)`
-- is accepted: `not r(X, Y)` with Y bound nowhere does not ask what it looks
-- like it asks, it asks whether `r` is EMPTY. Measured on three shapes, all
-- accepted. The obvious fix - fail it in `classify` - was tried and REVERTED,
-- because `safe` in src/engine.ts does not mean legal: an unsafe rule is not
-- rejected, it is made DEMAND-BACKED, so the check would move such a rule from
-- one evaluation strategy to another and leave the wrong answer where it was.
-- The diagnostic belongs where `unsafe/1` and `malformed/2` already live.
work(w_negation_range_restriction, "a variable bound only inside a negation is accepted, and asks whether the relation is empty").
work_state(w_negation_range_restriction, open).
