-- 55 - export_specifier, export_namespace_specifier.
work(w_export_specifier_forms, "export { a as b } and export * as ns: the two export forms with no node in this vocabulary").
-- DONE 2026-09-08. All eight cells answered: seven `handled` and one waiver,
-- and the waiver is the one the measurement earned rather than the one the
-- pattern suggested.
--
-- THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THE ITEM ASKED. Does the model resolve
-- `export { internal as external }` to `internal` or to `external`? To NEITHER.
-- Measured on a two-file probe before a rule was written: `exports_name`'s only
-- arm reads the declaration's `declaration` child, an `export { }` list has
-- none, and the probe returned exactly one row — the ordinary `export function
-- plain` beside it — while the cross-file call `usesShown -> hidden` was absent
-- from `calls_in` with its site reported as an unresolved `s_identifier`. The
-- model was SILENT, not wrong-named. That is the safe direction.
--
-- AND AT ONE LAYER IT WAS NOT SILENT AND NOT SAFE. A module whose whole export
-- surface is a specifier list had an EMPTY entry surface — `exported_fn` walks
-- the CONTAINMENT of an export declaration and a specifier list contains two
-- identifiers and no function — so `no_entry_point[audit]` fired on the file and
-- `may_not_be_reached` named a function that file exports AND calls. A smaller
-- seed reports live functions as maybe-dead, which is the direction
-- `export_kind_unseen[audit]` already exists to catch, arriving through a FORM
-- rather than through a misspelling.
--
-- THE ONE WAIVER: `export_namespace_specifier` at CONTROLFLOW. Measured from the
-- scanner rather than inherited from `export_all_declaration`'s row — an
-- ExportNamespaceSpecifier carries an `exported` child and NO `local` child at
-- all, so there is no name in this module for the entry surface to reach.
work_state(w_export_specifier_forms, done).

-- 56 - labeled_statement.
-- DONE 2026-09-08. THE ITEM'S NOTE CALLED IT "the one new statement form that
-- really does transfer control" AND THAT WAS THE HALF WORTH MEASURING. Measured
-- first, on a fixture built because the corpus held ZERO labels: with
-- `break outer` in the inner of two nested loops, `after_abrupt[code]` named
-- `pastBreak` and `pastContinue` - the siblings in the reference's OWN
-- statement list, which an unlabelled break kills too - and named none of
-- `pastLabelledBreak`, `pastLabelledContinue`, `pastLabelledBlock`. So the
-- control-flow layer WAS TREATING A LABELLED BREAK AS AN ORDINARY ONE, and the
-- one thing a label is for was invisible.
--
-- ONE OF THE THREE WAS NOT MERELY MISSING. A labelled LOOP body is a
-- `guard_kind` arm, so `pastLabelledBreak` was in `guarded` on other grounds
-- and the may-set was already hedging. A labelled BLOCK is not a loop, not an
-- arm and not a switch: `pastLabelledBlock` was in NEITHER `guarded` NOR
-- `may_not_run`, so the model positively said a statement runs which the only
-- path through its own block skips. That is the narrow direction, which this
-- layer documents as the dangerous one.
--
-- `r_labelled_boundary` reads the LABEL and not the keyword - `label_ref` joins
-- through the `label` child, which an unlabelled break does not have - so
-- `beyondPlainBreak` stays out, and the walk stops at the `labeled_statement`,
-- so `beyondLabel` and `afterBlock` stay out too. `label_boundary` is a SIXTH
-- mechanism rather than a sixth kind filed under `abrupt`, because a label
-- receives a transfer instead of performing one.
work(w_labelled_control, "DONE 2026-09-08: r_labelled_boundary; a labelled statement, and break/continue to a label - the one new statement form that really does transfer control").
work_state(w_labelled_control, done).
work_spawned(w_labelled_control, f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, callgraph,    w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, dataflow,     w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, controlflow,  w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, modules,      w_labelled_control).

-- 57 - empty_statement, debugger_statement, with_statement.
-- EIGHT OF TWELVE CLOSED 2026-09-08, and the four that are not are the item.
-- Their claims are the only ones left below: a closed cell whose claim stays
-- queued under an item that is not done is `queue_stale[audit]`, and the
-- convention this file already follows (see w_cf_accessor's optional twin) is
-- that the claim goes when the cell does.
--
-- THE NOTE SAID "no layer is LIKELY to have an opinion" AND THE CONTROL-FLOW
-- LAYER HAS ONE. Measured with the fixture in the corpus: of four
-- `empty_statement` nodes THREE are `guarded[code]`, TWO are a `guard_arm`
-- (`while (total < 0) ;` and an `if` consequent) and ONE is `after_abrupt`;
-- of two `debugger_statement` nodes one is guarded and one is after an abrupt
-- transfer. An inert statement OCCUPIES a conditional position; it does not
-- CREATE one, which is the claim the cell asks about, and the two are different
-- sentences. The other three layers were asked the same way and answered zero
-- with a positive control on every query - see facts/js-statements.rofl.
--
-- `with_statement` IS OPEN AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED.
-- scanners/js_ast.ts calls babel with `sourceType: 'module'` and nothing else;
-- a module is strict; `with (o) { p(); }` comes back
--
--     ast_parse_error[code]("with_mod.js", "'with' in strict mode. (1:0)")
--
-- with `nodes = 0` and an EMPTY kind set. The refusal is total, so a `with`
-- anywhere in a fixture does not merely fail to produce a node - it deletes
-- that whole file from the corpus. THAT IS A SETTING AND NOT A CONSTRAINT, and
-- the distance was measured rather than assumed, which is the correction
-- f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language paid for one
-- component over: the SAME source under `sourceType: 'script'` parses and emits
-- `WithStatement`, and under `sourceType: 'unambiguous'` babel chooses `script`
-- for it while choosing `module` for ALL FIVE existing corpus files - measured
-- file by file, so the change would leave the present corpus byte-identical.
--
-- SO THE CELLS ARE WORKABLE AND ARE LEFT UNBLOCKED ON PURPOSE. What they are
-- waiting for is not a parser but a decision plus the analysis that comes with
-- it: `with` REDIRECTS NAME RESOLUTION at run time, so every identifier in its
-- body may name a property of the object instead of the binder the value layer
-- resolves it to. `sees_binder`, `may_be_node` and every call edge that rests
-- on them would be reporting a binding the language does not guarantee. That is
-- an unsoundness at the dataflow and call-graph layers, not an inert statement,
-- and it is why this is left as work rather than waived at four layers on the
-- strength of the word "inert". See f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard.
work(w_inert_statements, "empty and debugger are answered at four layers each; `with` is open because the scanner parses every file as a module - a setting, measured - and because it redirects name resolution").
work_state(w_inert_statements, open).
work_spawned(w_inert_statements, f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard).
-- THE NOTE'S ABSENCE CLAIM, AS A QUERY, 2026-09-09 (w_note_is_not_evidence).
-- `with` is open because no fixture can hold one: the scanner parses every file
-- as a module and `with` is a syntax error in module code. The control is a
-- KIND OF THE LANGUAGE rather than a name somebody typed - `call_expression`
-- cannot leave a call corpus without every other gate here going red first - so
-- it does not expire the way a mutant anchored to authored text does.
witness_absent(w_inert_statements, w_js_corpus,
               "ast_node[code](N, with_statement, F, L)",
               "ast_node[code](N, call_expression, F, L)").
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, callgraph,    w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, dataflow,     w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, controlflow,  w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, modules,      w_inert_statements).

-- 58 - directive, directive_literal, interpreter_directive.
-- DONE 2026-09-08. Twelve cells, twelve waivers, and the item's own premise was
-- the first thing refuted: it asks whether the scanner drops directives and it
-- does not. babel hangs a directive prologue off a `directives` ARRAY and a
-- hashbang off `interpreter`, scanners/js_ast.ts walks every own property, so
-- all three kinds arrive with their values on the store. Nothing needed fixing
-- in the scanner; the question was what the model does with facts it has.
--
-- THE NEGATIVE THAT CARRIES ALL TWELVE IS ONE QUERY, not twelve. The kernel
-- reifies rule bodies, so `slot_atom(ast_node, 2, K)` is the set of node kinds
-- any rule NAMES (52 across the eight js rule packs) and `slot_atom(ast_child, 2, F)`
-- the set of fields any rule READS (34). None of `directive`,
-- `directive_literal`, `interpreter_directive` is in the first; neither
-- `directives` nor `interpreter` is in the second; `string_literal` and `body`
-- are the positive controls. Twelve empty membership queries would each have
-- been a fact about the query.
--
-- FOUR THINGS MEASURED THAT THE PLAN DID NOT EXPECT:
--   * `abrupt_kind(directive)` - the most aggressive false claim this layer can
--     make - moves NOTHING. `abrupt_at` wants a node at an index of a
--     `stmt_seq_field` (`body` or `consequent`) and a directive is in
--     `directives`, so the model cannot place one in statement order at all.
--     A survivor, and asserted as one.
--   * ...but `guard_kind(directive, value)` moves `guarded` 428 -> 431, so the
--     layer is not blind to the node and the waiver is a decision.
--   * `literal_kind(directive_literal)` DERIVES three `may_be_lit` rows. The
--     first draft of the fact pack said it derived none; the mutant refuted the
--     comment before it was committed. No answer moves - a value with no
--     consumer rather than a value the model cannot compute.
--   * AND THE ERA TABLE IS WRONG ABOUT THE HASHBANG. See the finding: `#!` is
--     the ES2023 Hashbang Grammar and `kind_baseline(js, interpreter_directive)`
--     says es5 takes it; stating the true year puts the feature in
--     `feature_unreachable[audit]` because the scale stops at ts5/2022. The row
--     stays, annotated, because both answers are false and only an `es2023` on
--     the scale fixes it - which is the owner's.
work(w_directives, "'use strict' and the hashbang: directives are a real construct and the scanner emits them").
work_state(w_directives, done).
work_spawned(w_directives, f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it).
work_spawned(w_directives, f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, modules,      w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, modules,      w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, modules,      w_directives).

-- 59 - THREE KINDS THE VOCABULARY DID NOT TAKE ON, each for a MEASURED
-- reason rather than a preference, and all three wait on the same two things:
-- a parser plugin and an environment.
--   class_accessor_property   the scanner returns NO node at all for
--                             `accessor a = 1` without the decorators plugin
--                             STALE 2026-09-09: the plugin is on since the
--                             decorator work and the corpus holds two of them.
--                             See the witness rows under the item below.
--   import_attribute          scannable today, but `import attributes` is
--                             ES2025 and the highest environment on the scale
--                             is ts5 at 2022, so the feature would sit in
--                             `feature_unreachable[audit]` for ever
--                             DETAIL CORRECTED 2026-09-09, conclusion intact:
--                             the highest rank on the scale is `es2023` at 2023,
--                             not ts5 at 2022 — `env_rank(es2023, 2023)` is in
--                             facts/js-env.rofl. 2023 is still short of 2025, so
--                             the feature is still unreachable and the item's
--                             answer does not move. Recorded because a wrong
--                             detail under a right conclusion is the shape that
--                             survives review: nobody re-reads the premise of a
--                             sentence they agree with.
--                             AND THE UNBLOCKER MAY NOT BE THE ERA SCALE AT ALL.
--                             Import attributes ship in Node 22. The owner
--                             approved a SECOND axis on 2026-09-09 — runtime
--                             versions as subkinds, `runtime_includes(node20,
--                             node18)`, bridged to the ES scale by
--                             `provides_release(node20, es2022)` — so an
--                             environment could reach this feature through the
--                             runtime axis without the ECMAScript scale moving
--                             at all. If that lands, this third of the item
--                             closes for a different reason than the one written
--                             here, and the owner should be told which.
--   export_default_specifier  `export v from` is a Babel proposal and NO
--                             environment claims it, not even ts5
-- The first two land with the decorator work, which needs the same plugin and
-- will need the same question asked of the environment scale.
work(w_plugin_gated_kinds, "class_accessor_property, import_attribute and export_default_specifier: the PLUGIN HALF IS DISCHARGED - the corpus holds two class_accessor_property nodes since the decorator work turned decoratorAutoAccessors on - and what is left for all three is an environment the era scale does not have").
work_state(w_plugin_gated_kinds, open).
-- THE NOTE WAS REFUTED BY ITS OWN WITNESS, 2026-09-09 (w_note_is_not_evidence),
-- and this is the SEVENTH note in this file refuted by a first probe. It is
-- also the first one found BY THE MECHANISM rather than by a probe written from
-- the note, which is the whole difference the item was for: a probe written
-- from a note agrees with the note by construction.
--
-- The line above used to read `the scanner returns NO node at all for
-- `accessor a = 1` without the decorators plugin`. Every word of that is true
-- and the conclusion drawn from it - that the kind waits on a plugin - stopped
-- being true on 2026-09-08, when the decorator work added `decorators` and
-- `decoratorAutoAccessors` to `PARSER_PLUGINS` for its own reasons. Nobody
-- re-read this note, because a note is prose and nothing watches prose. Asked
-- of the corpus world: TWO nodes, shapes.ts lines 465 and 837, both written by
-- the decorator item itself.
--
-- So the witness is a FLOOR and it says the discharged half out loud. It is a
-- floor rather than the count 2 because a fixture gaining a third accessor must
-- not redden a claim about whether the parser emits the kind at all.
witness_in_atleast(w_plugin_gated_kinds, w_js_corpus,
                   "ast_node[code](N, class_accessor_property, F, L)", 1).
-- ...and the two that ARE still absent, each with the same live control, so the
-- remaining half of the item is a row rather than a sentence. Both are waiting
-- on the ERA SCALE, which tops out at ts5/2022: `import attributes` is ES2025
-- and `export v from` is a Babel proposal no environment claims.
witness_absent(w_plugin_gated_kinds, w_js_corpus,
               "ast_node[code](N, import_attribute, F, L)",
               "ast_node[code](N, call_expression, F, L)").
witness_absent(w_plugin_gated_kinds, w_js_corpus,
               "ast_node[code](N, export_default_specifier, F, L)",
               "ast_node[code](N, call_expression, F, L)").


-- ==========================================================================
-- 60 - THE THREE CELLS ITEM 50 COULD NOT CLOSE, plus one it had to REOPEN.
--
-- MEASURED AT RUN TIME, seven cases, recording which functions ran: an array
-- pattern over a custom iterable runs the iterator; an object spread over an
-- object with a getter runs the getter; an array spread and a spread ARGUMENT
-- both run the iterator; an object rest runs the source`s getters. So
-- `a_no_control_transfer` is FALSE for all three, and it was already false for
-- `object_pattern`, which carried that reason from the forty-cell sweep - its
-- row is gone from facts/js-controlflow.rofl and its cell is claimed here.
--
-- THIS IS `accessor_call` WEARING ANOTHER SYNTAX. The layer already models one
-- hidden call - `transfer_mechanism(member_expression, accessor_call)` reached
-- by `accessor_read` - and every case above is the same shape: a construct
-- whose surface is a binding and whose semantics is a call. What it needs is
-- the receiver`s ITERATOR, which is a standard-library question, so this waits
-- on the same chain `w_effect_layer` does.
--
-- `assignment_pattern` IS NOT HERE and that is the discriminating half: it was
-- closable with the machinery this layer already has, because its transfer is
-- an arm skipped by a condition rather than a call the syntax hides.
-- DONE 2026-09-08, and the item was blocked on THE WRONG THING. It waited on
-- `w_env_api_surface` because naming the receiver`s iterator reads as a
-- standard-library question. MEASURED against the surface that item generated,
-- with a positive control: `lib_member(P, K, R)` carries 130 rows over eight
-- prototypes and `lib_member(P, "iterator", R)` is ZERO, as are the
-- `"Symbol.iterator"` and `"next"` spellings — the surface is keyed by NAME and
-- a well-known symbol is not a name. So the dependency could never have lifted
-- this item and `work_needs` is REMOVED rather than ticked off.
--
-- WHAT ACTUALLY BLOCKED IT WAS THE CORPUS. Ten cases run against V8`s stack:
-- over a PLAIN array or a PLAIN object all four syntaxes run NO USER CODE, and
-- every destructuring site in this corpus read a plain source — so nothing here
-- could contradict `a_no_control_transfer` and the layer stood on a sentence.
-- Over a source owning a getter or a `[Symbol.iterator]` each one transfers, and
-- both hops are attributed to the enclosing function exactly as `for-of`'s are.
-- The machinery was ALREADY THERE: `accessor_of[flow]` names the getter and
-- `member_value[flow](Obj, "iterator", M)` names the iterator, which is what
-- `for_of_iterates` has read since 2026-09-07.
--
-- `assignment_pattern` STAYS THE DISCRIMINATING HALF and it stayed right: its
-- transfer is an arm skipped by a condition and it needed `guard_kind`, while
-- these four needed the call graph. What was wrong was the reason the others
-- were held back, not the line drawn between them.
work(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, "an array pattern, an object rest and every spread position transfer control into user code - an iterator or a getter - and the layer`s reason atom for all four says they transfer nothing").
work_state(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, done).
claim(queued, js, object_pattern,              none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, array_pattern,               none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, rest_element,                none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, spread_element,              none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
work_spawned(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does).

-- 2026-09-08. The last pin in test/worklist.test.ts that is still a number, and
-- the only assertion there whose SUBJECT is the size of the open set. Three
-- parallel branches moved it from 102 to 70 in one afternoon; a fourth would
-- have moved it again, and no branch can know the merged value. Whether it can
-- be stated as an identity - the open set against a relation derived some other
-- way - is a real question and not a tidy-up.
-- DONE 2026-09-09, and NOT as an identity. The question the item asked - can
-- the open set be stated against a relation derived some other way - was
-- answered by the set itself getting SMALL AND STABLE for a reason: 15 -> 6,
-- and all six are cells whose work is known and whose DECISION is the owner's
-- (with_statement at four layers, `require`, the subpath specifier). So the
-- pin is now a deepEqual over the six rows by name. That is stronger than an
-- identity would have been for the merge problem this item was entered for -
-- two branches closing different cells produce two different SETS, and git
-- merges a set of named rows where it cannot merge two numbers - and it is
-- honest about what it is: an enumeration that must be re-read, not a law.
-- WHAT WAS NOT DONE: no second derivation of the open set exists, so nothing
-- CROSS-CHECKS the enumeration. If the six ever grow back into a backlog, the
-- identity question returns with them.
work(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, "DONE 2026-09-09: the open-cell pin is a named set of six rows, every one of them the owner's to decide; an identity was not needed once the set stopped being a backlog").
work_state(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, done).
work_spawned(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, f_an_instruments_world_is_part_of_its_claim).

-- ==========================================================================
-- 62 - WHAT A DECORATOR DOES TO THE THING IT DECORATES.
--
-- The CALL is modelled (r_decorator_call, 2026-09-08) and the RESULT is not.
-- A decorator returns a replacement: `@decoOnce class Decorated {}` binds
-- whatever `decoOnce` gave back, so the value layer's answer for `Decorated`
-- should be the decorator's return and today it is the class. Same for a
-- decorated method and for an auto-accessor, whose get/set pair a decorator may
-- replace wholesale.
--
-- IT IS A VALUE QUESTION AND NOT A CALL QUESTION, which is why it is a separate
-- item rather than a loose end on the one that landed: `returns[flow]` already
-- carries what a function gave back, so the join exists; what is missing is a
-- rule saying that a decorated NAME evaluates to it.
--
-- AND `class_accessor_property` IS HERE FOR A DIFFERENT REASON. Its callgraph
-- and dataflow cells are open because the corpus has a DECLARATION and no READ:
-- `accessor slot = 4` synthesises a getter and a setter, and nothing in this
-- corpus calls them. The control-flow cell is waived on the measured ground
-- that both accessors are language-created, so no transfer into USER code
-- happens - which is exactly what makes the other two cells about the value
-- layer rather than about the call graph.
-- DONE 2026-09-08, and the three cells closed for two different reasons that
-- the item had already told apart. `r_decorator_replacement` in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl says a decorated NAME and a decorated METHOD evaluate
-- to what the decorator returned; `class_field_kind(class_accessor_property)`
-- is one ROW that puts an auto-accessor into `field_of`, which answers both of
-- its cells at once.
--
-- THE FIXTURE CAME FIRST FOR BOTH, and in both cases because MEASURING said the
-- existing sites could decide nothing:
--
--   * `may_be_node` of BOTH decorator nodes in the corpus was EMPTY.
--     `decoOnce` and `decoApplied` return a PARAMETER, and a decorator call has
--     no argument nodes in the grammar at all, so `arg_at` has nothing to bind.
--     A replacement rule written against those two would have derived nothing
--     while looking finished.
--   * `accessor slot = 4` had been in the corpus since decorators landed and
--     nothing read it, which is what the note below already said.
--
-- MEASURED AFTER: over the whole corpus the edge set gains exactly three edges
-- and loses none - `usesSwage -> spliced`, `usesThimble -> crimped`,
-- `hauls -> slung` - and `accessor_read` does not move, which is the check that
-- the accessor row cannot manufacture a getter call by accident.
--
-- WHAT IS LEFT AND IS NOT THIS ITEM: a decorator returns a DIFFERENT SHAPE for
-- every position it can occupy - a value for a class or a method, an
-- initialiser transformer for a field, a `{get, set, init}` triple for an
-- auto-accessor - so the two arms here cover the two positions whose return IS
-- the replacement and refuse the other two on purpose. Recorded as
-- f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position.
work(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, "DONE 2026-09-08: r_decorator_replacement gives a decorated name and a decorated method the decorator's return, and an auto-accessor becomes a field").
work_state(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, done).
work_spawned(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position).


-- ==========================================================================
-- 63 - WHAT AN `await` CALLS, AND WHERE THE RUNTIME PUTS IT.
--
-- The last cell of w_cg_invisible_calls, re-pointed twice on 2026-09-08 and
-- landing here because BOTH of its earlier owners were wrong about it.
--
-- IT WENT TO w_env_api_surface FIRST, correctly by one of its two reasons:
-- `await x` calls `Promise.prototype.then`, a library method with no node in
-- this program. That item closed the same afternoon, in a parallel branch that
-- could not see the re-pointing, and `false_done[audit]` reported the pair
-- within one run - a done item owning an open cell.
--
-- AND THE LIBRARY REASON IS NOT THE BINDING ONE. Measured:
-- `member_value[flow](O, "then", V)` is ZERO over the whole corpus, with the
-- positive control `member_value[flow](O, "next", V)` at two rows out of 140
-- distinct keys - so the model does not know an await`s callee at all, and
-- `stdlib_member[audit]` never sees it. A USER thenable would put a real node
-- there, which is what makes this not simply `no_source_target` like
-- `array.join`. AND THEN V8 ATTRIBUTES THAT NODE`S CALL TO `<top>`, measured a
-- week earlier when the suspension work asked where a resumption runs: so a
-- model that drew `caller -> then` would be contradicted by the acceptance
-- oracle, and one that drew `<top> -> then` would be right for a reason that
-- has nothing to do with the await.
--
-- SO THE CELL IS OPEN ON A QUESTION AND NOT ON A GAP: what does this layer mean
-- by a caller, when the language performs the call and the runtime credits the
-- microtask queue. `w_env_api_surface` answered the half about the callee`s
-- name; nobody has answered the half about the caller`s.
work(w_await_is_a_call_the_oracle_places_elsewhere, "DONE 2026-09-08: BOTH stated reasons were false and the item is its own answer - an await calls no library method at all, and the oracle does not contradict a thenable`s `then`, it DROPS the frame because a caller-less edge carries no file").
work_state(w_await_is_a_call_the_oracle_places_elsewhere, done).
work_spawned(w_await_is_a_call_the_oracle_places_elsewhere, f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller).

-- 64 - A BODY THAT IS CONNECTED AND BADLY ORDERED, which is exactly what
-- w_join_planner could not take on. The cross-product planner reacts to a
-- structural fact and has no opinion about a body whose every literal shares a
-- variable with the ones before it. The measured instance is `closer_v`:
-- `encloses_v(F, X), encloses_v(G, X), ast_within(F, G)` and
-- `encloses_v(F, X), ast_within(F, G), encloses_v(G, X)` are both connected
-- throughout, and the second costs the control-flow world +117% - 3 353 932 rows
-- to 7 285 630. A human reverted it by hand on 2026-09-09 and nothing in the
-- tree would have stopped it being written again.
--
-- WHAT IT NEEDS IS NOT MORE STATISTICS OF THE SAME KIND, and that is the whole
-- content of the item. Per-relation cardinality and per-position distinct counts
-- were measured, off a FINISHED store, and they got this case right and two
-- unmeasured cases wrong by up to 4.3x. The quantity that decides it is the
-- fan-out of a probe under the values a JOIN supplies, which is a per-join
-- histogram rather than a per-relation average - or, the other way round, an
-- ADAPTIVE order read off what the engine actually handed out on the last round,
-- which this engine can see and no static model can.
work(w_connected_and_badly_ordered, "a body whose literals are all connected can still be in the wrong order, and the only planner that could tell needs per-join statistics rather than per-relation ones").
work_state(w_connected_and_badly_ordered, open).
-- RE-MEASURED 2026-09-09 AGAINST ANOTHER SESSION'S EVIDENCE, and the item got
-- sharper rather than smaller. Over 16 files of eslint/lib they measured that a
-- position-0 premise's observed cost equals a STATIC count conditioned on its
-- written argument pattern - 68.8% exactly non-recursive, 28.9% recursive,
-- against a 23.1% shuffled control. The half that transfers transfers cleanly:
-- on THIS corpus `encloses_v` is 5 815 rows and `encloses_s` is 133, so the
-- conditioned count does separate `closer_v` from `closer_s`, which no property
-- of their identical text could. THE POLICY DOES NOT FOLLOW FROM THE ESTIMATE:
-- `fn_node` is 395 rows here and the return statements 400, and `has_return`'s
-- -23.3% repair LEADS WITH THE LARGER - so a rule that seeds with the cheapest
-- conditioned count makes no repair at all, and on another corpus would make it
-- on a 1.3% gap. What decides that body is the DIRECTION of the second literal,
-- not either seed's size. So this item wants the count ON THE JOIN.
work_needs(w_connected_and_badly_ordered, w_join_planner).

-- ==========================================================================
-- 67 - THE RUNTIME SURFACE. DONE 2026-09-09.
--
-- `w_env_api_surface` gave the model the LANGUAGE's standard library: 130
-- prototype methods read from TypeScript's own lib.es*.d.ts, dated by the
-- edition that introduced them. It could not say anything about `console.log`,
-- `fs.readFileSync` or `fetch`, because none of those is ECMAScript at all.
-- This item is the HOST: what a program reaches for that is neither in the
-- program nor in the language.
--
-- WHAT WAS BUILT, and from which source:
--   scanners/host_lib.ts          the generator, via the TypeScript CHECKER
--   facts/js-host-surface.rofl    GENERATED: 939 globals across two hosts,
--                                 57 node modules, 1 321 members, 718 dated,
--                                 26 deprecated, 17 with a named replacement
--   facts/js-host.rofl            AUTHORED: the runtime axis, the bridge, and
--                                 the effect attribution
--   rules/js-host.rofl            the two doors, the four site shapes, the
--                                 composition, and eleven audits
--   test/fixtures/js-host/        a corpus of its own — see NO SITE below
--   test/js-host.test.ts          31 tests, ten mutants, one declared survivor
--
-- THE CORPUS HAD NO SITE AND THAT WAS MEASURED FIRST. Over every module
-- specifier in test/fixtures/js-call: `./alpha.mjs` x6, `./gamma.mjs` x4,
-- `./trace.mjs` x3, `./delta.mjs` x3, `./x` x1 — FIVE distinct specifiers, all
-- relative, NOT ONE node builtin. Grepped for console, fetch, setTimeout,
-- setInterval, queueMicrotask, structuredClone, URL, TextEncoder,
-- AbortController, performance, process, Buffer, require and globalThis: every
-- hit is inside a COMMENT. So the layer would have derived exactly zero rows
-- there, and the fixture lives in test/fixtures/js-host/ rather than as a sixth
-- file in the call corpus — which would have moved every named set and every
-- cost pin in the suite for a layer that needs none of them.
--
-- THE TWO AXES, ON THE OWNER'S DESIGN OF 2026-09-09. A runtime version is a
-- SUBKIND of a runtime family, so `runtime_includes` is `includes` and
-- `runtime_reaches` is `reaches`, walked the same way — no second traversal
-- exists. `provides_release(node18, es2022)` is the BRIDGE, and it lands on a
-- `release/1` that scanners/ts_lib.ts already generates, so nothing on the era
-- scale is minted here. THE BRIDGE BITES: `[3,1,2].toSorted()` is es2023, node18
-- provides es2022, and `runtime_lib_unsupported[audit](node18, C, array,
-- "toSorted")` is the corpus's one row — the two axes answering one question.
--
-- AND THE COMPOSITION IS CHECKED AGAINST THE COMPARISON. `has_api[audit]`,
-- walked over `runtime_includes`, must equal `arrived_by[code]`, computed by one
-- `<=` over the semver in `@since`. 1 924 rows, EQUAL, asserted as a set
-- identity rather than a count.
--
-- THE SHAPE THE EFFECT LAYER JOINS AGAINST is `host_call_effect[audit](C, E,
-- Why)` — a call site, a Koka atom, and the PROVENANCE (`by_member`,
-- `by_module`, `by_global`), because an effect attributed to a member by name
-- and one inherited from its module's default are not equally strong. No rule
-- downstream has to recognise a spelling. `host_effect_atom/1` is declared so a
-- misspelling is a row; the LATTICE is not declared here and must not be.
work(w_runtime_surface, "DONE 2026-09-09: the runtime surface of two hosts generated from @types/node and lib.dom.d.ts, a second version axis as subkinds of a runtime family, the bridge provides_release to the ES releases, and host_call_effect as the row the effect layer joins").
work_state(w_runtime_surface, done).
work_needs(w_runtime_surface, w_env_api_surface).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_the_rule_shape_census_is_a_generated_pack_with_no_gate).
work_spawned(w_runtime_surface, f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways).

-- NO WITNESS, AND THE REASON IS THE INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN THE CLAIM.
-- `w_note_is_not_evidence` says a witness names its world, and every sentence
-- above is about a world `facts/worlds.rofl` does not declare: the runtime world
-- of test/js-host-world.ts. Declaring a fourth world is possible and was
-- deliberately not done, for two measured reasons. FIRST,
-- scripts/witness_check.ts builds a world from `world_pack` plus `world_corpus`
-- and asserts the SCANNED AST only — it emits none of the string-cutting host
-- facts (`str_scheme`, `str_seg`) that rules/js-modules.rofl needs, so
-- `resolved_builtin` would be empty there and every module-door claim would
-- witness as 0 while being true. SECOND, test/note-witness.test.ts pins the
-- worlds a witness may name as the NAMED SET {w_js_corpus, w_ledger, w_queue},
-- and moving it is a change to a file three other branches read. THE CLAIMS ARE
-- CHECKED IN test/js-host.test.ts INSTEAD, 31 tests, and the fourth world is
-- left as a proposal with its blocker stated rather than as an absence.

-- ...AND A MEASUREMENT OWED TO ITEM 59. `w_plugin_gated_kinds` says
-- `import_attribute` is "scannable today, but `import attributes` is ES2025 and
-- the highest environment on the scale is ts5 at 2022, so the feature would sit
-- in `feature_unreachable[audit]` for ever". RE-MEASURED 2026-09-09 against the
-- scanner: `import cfg from "./c.json" with { type: "json" }` PARSES with the
-- current plugin list and emits an `import_attribute` node — so the SCANNABLE
-- half of that sentence is confirmed rather than stale, unlike the
-- `class_accessor_property` half which was corrected the same day.
--
-- AND THIS ITEM DOES NOT UNBLOCK IT, which is worth saying because a second
-- version axis looks as though it should. Reachability for a syntax feature is
-- decided by `env_has[audit]`, which reads `environment/1` and `provides/2` on
-- the ERA scale; this item added neither and could not, because the era scale is
-- the owner's. What it DID add is the mechanism that would make the answer
-- cheap once an environment exists: `runtime_reaches_release[audit](node22,
-- es2024)` is a live row, so a runtime already reaches an ES release by
-- composition. The missing piece is one `environment` at es2025 plus a
-- `provides(es2025, import_attributes)`, and both are the owner's to write.

-- ==========================================================================
-- THE EFFECT LAYER, 2026-09-09 (w_effect_layer). `layer(effect).` opened one
-- hundred cells and this block is where the eighteen it could not close are
-- owned. Read facts/js-effects.rofl for the verdicts and rules/js-effects.rofl
-- for the lattice; what is here is only the ownership, which is what
-- `open_cell[audit]` demands and what makes the declaration a valid state of
-- the queue rather than a hundred rows nobody answers for.
--
-- THE FOUR ITEMS BELOW ARE FOUR DIFFERENT REASONS AND NOT ONE REASON FOUR
-- TIMES, which is the whole point of splitting them: one waits on a pack two
-- other agents are writing, one is a rule this layer does not have, one is a
-- hidden call in an operator, and one is an edge across the module boundary.
-- Grouping them by their symptom - "the effect is unknown" - is the queue smell
-- `f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours` names.

-- 65 - THE AMBIENT SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONE THIS LAYER CANNOT WRITE ITSELF.
-- 261 of this corpus's call sites do not resolve to a node in the program, and
-- a call whose callee is outside the program has an effect nothing here can
-- state. `console.log(...)` is `io` only if something says `console` is a
-- runtime surface; `Math.max(...)` is `total` and `Math.random()` is `ndet`,
-- and the two differ by the MEMBER rather than by the surface.
--
-- WHAT THIS ITEM NEEDS, stated as three relations rather than as a wish, and
-- all three are declared `edb` in facts/js-effects.rofl so that a world with no
-- surface reads them as empty rather than as a misspelling:
--
--   ambient_binding(File, Name, Surface)     which free NAME is which surface
--   surface_origin(Surface, Origin)          which declaration file it came
--                                            from: es_intrinsic (lib.es*.d.ts),
--                                            host_runtime (lib.dom.d.ts or
--                                            @types/node), builtin_prototype
--   ambient_effect(Surface, Member, Effect)  the map into the lattice, ONE ROW
--                                            PER MEMBER, naming an `eff_name`
--
-- THE FIRST TWO ARE FREE AND THE THIRD IS NOT, and saying which is which is
-- most of this item. A declaration file fixes the surface and the member name
-- with no judgement at all; NOTHING in a `.d.ts` signature says `readFileSync`
-- reads and `writeFileSync` writes. So `ambient_effect` is a table somebody has
-- to build member by member, and until it exists every ambient call is this
-- open cell rather than a label - which is the honest answer to the majority
-- case and the reason `ndet` has no seed anywhere in the rules.
--
-- MEASURED TODAY, so the item has a floor rather than a hope: ten call sites
-- already carry a derived (surface, operation) pair with no `.d.ts` involved at
-- all - `array:join` twice, `array:at`, `string:concat`, `string:replaceAll`,
-- `string:substr`, `string:trimLeft`, `regexp:test` twice, `bigint:toString` -
-- because `prototype_of[flow]` reads the receiver's KIND and `builtin_prototype`
-- names the six the language owns. Eight distinct pairs, `ambient_effect` empty,
-- so `concrete_unmapped[flow]` is eight rows and that is the in-tray.
--
-- AND IT IS WHAT DECIDES `axis_applies(shape, effect)`. The concrete column
-- earns a refinement column exactly when one member is attributed and one is
-- not - one `handled` shape beside one `not_yet` shape is what
-- `axis_earns[audit]` asks for. Today all ten would carry the same reason, so
-- declaring the row would multiply the table and answer nothing, which is what
-- `unearned_axis[audit]` exists to refuse. test/js-effects.test.ts plants the
-- attribution as a positive control, so that sentence is runnable.
work(w_effect_ambient_call, "the effect of a call whose callee is not in this program: the (surface, operation) pair is derivable and the map into the lattice is a table the runtime and globals surfaces owe, one row per member").
work_state(w_effect_ambient_call, open).
work_spawned(w_effect_ambient_call, f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free).
claim(queued, js, call_expression,          none, effect, w_effect_ambient_call).
claim(queued, js, optional_call_expression, none, effect, w_effect_ambient_call).
claim(queued, js, new_expression,           none, effect, w_effect_ambient_call).
-- `identifier` is the same question one level down and NOT a fifth call kind: a
-- bare name is a local (bottom, or `read<local>` if something reassigns it), an
-- import (bottom), or a free global (`read<global>`, and a ReferenceError if it
-- is not there). The rules derive the first two today; only the third needs the
-- surface, and `ambient_binding` is exactly the relation that decides it.
claim(queued, js, identifier,               none, effect, w_effect_ambient_call).

-- 66 - WHAT RUNS WHEN A CLASS IS DEFINED. A static block and a field
-- initialiser run at DECLARATION time, not at a call, so their effect belongs
-- to the declaration site - and `eff_latent[flow]` has no arm for it, because
-- the propagation rule hangs on a `resolves` row and there is no call site
-- here to hang it on.
--
-- IT IS WHY `class_declaration` IS NOT IN THE LATENT GROUP. The other four
-- definition kinds - `function_declaration`, `class_method`,
-- `class_private_method`, `object_method` - really do perform nothing where
-- they are written. A class declaration does: it evaluates `extends`, it runs
-- every static block, and it runs every static field initialiser, in order.
-- `static_block_of[flow]` and `field_of[flow]` already name the pieces; what is
-- missing is the join from them to the declaration.
work(w_effect_class_initialisers, "a static block and a field initialiser run when the class is DEFINED, so their latent effect belongs to the declaration site and the propagation rule has no call site to hang it on").
work_state(w_effect_class_initialisers, open).
claim(queued, js, class_declaration,        none, effect, w_effect_class_initialisers).
claim(queued, js, class_property,           none, effect, w_effect_class_initialisers).
claim(queued, js, class_private_property,   none, effect, w_effect_class_initialisers).
claim(queued, js, class_accessor_property,  none, effect, w_effect_class_initialisers).
claim(queued, js, static_block,             none, effect, w_effect_class_initialisers).

-- 67 - AN OPERATOR THAT IS A CALL. `a + b` runs `valueOf` or `toString` on an
-- operand that has one and throws a TypeError for a Symbol; `-x` coerces;
-- a template interpolation coerces. This is `accessor_call` wearing a third
-- syntax - a construct whose surface is an operator and whose semantics is a
-- call - and it is the same shape `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` found in a
-- binding.
--
-- WHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY THE AMBIENT ITEM. The conversion method is a member of
-- the OPERAND's prototype, so half the cases are answerable from
-- `prototype_of[flow]` with no surface pack at all - `"a" + "b"` cannot call
-- anything user-written - and the other half need to know whether the operand's
-- object declares `valueOf`, which `member_value[flow]` already answers for an
-- in-program object. So this is a rule this layer could have and does not, and
-- it is not waiting on anybody.
--
-- THE OPERATOR IS ALREADY IN THE FACTS: `ast_attr(N, operator, V)` is 233 rows
-- over this corpus with thirteen distinct operators, and it separates the cases
-- - `===` never coerces, `<` always may, `typeof` is total and `delete` is a
-- WRITE. Two of those four have no site here (`typeof`, `delete`), which is
-- what the item has to answer for before it writes a rule.
work(w_effect_implicit_coercion, "a binary operator, a unary operator and a template interpolation all call a conversion method on their operand: an operator whose semantics is a call, which is accessor_call in a third syntax").
work_state(w_effect_implicit_coercion, open).
claim(queued, js, binary_expression,        none, effect, w_effect_implicit_coercion).
claim(queued, js, unary_expression,         none, effect, w_effect_implicit_coercion).
claim(queued, js, template_literal,         none, effect, w_effect_implicit_coercion).

-- 68 - AN IMPORT EVALUATES A MODULE. `import './m.js'` runs m's whole top
-- level, so its effect is m's top-level effect, and nothing joins one file's
-- top level to another's. This is a MISSING EDGE and not a missing surface,
-- which is what distinguishes it from item 65: the module is in the corpus, the
-- modules layer already resolves the specifier to it, and
-- `w_cg_module_boundary` already crossed the boundary for a NAME. Crossing it
-- for a top-level EFFECT is the same join one relation over.
--
-- FIVE KINDS AND NOT TWO. `export * from` and the `export ... from` form of
-- `export_named_declaration` evaluate the source module exactly as an import
-- does; `import()` evaluates it too and returns a promise, so it carries the
-- module's effect on top of whatever the dynamic form costs.
work(w_effect_module_evaluation, "a static import EVALUATES the imported module, so an import's effect is that module's top-level effect - a missing edge across a boundary the modules layer already crosses for a name").
work_state(w_effect_module_evaluation, open).
claim(queued, js, import_declaration,       none, effect, w_effect_module_evaluation).
claim(queued, js, import,                   none, effect, w_effect_module_evaluation).
claim(queued, js, import_expression,        none, effect, w_effect_module_evaluation).
claim(queued, js, export_all_declaration,   none, effect, w_effect_module_evaluation).
claim(queued, js, export_named_declaration, none, effect, w_effect_module_evaluation).

-- ...AND THE FIFTH `with_statement` CELL GOES TO THE ITEM THAT ALREADY HOLDS
-- THE OTHER FOUR. `with (o) { x }` turns every bare name in its body into a
-- member read on `o`, so it changes what a READ MEANS - which is this layer's
-- `read<h>` seed, and it is the same decision about name resolution the other
-- four cells wait on rather than a new one.
claim(queued, js, with_statement,           none, effect, w_inert_statements).

-- 69 - THE SWEEP, AND IT IS DONE BEFORE IT IS OFFERED. Every other layer has a
-- sweeping item that owns whatever the named items leave behind; this one has
-- nothing to sweep, because the eighteen open cells were all claimed by name in
-- the same commit that declared the layer. The row exists so that the layer's
-- residue is EXPLICITLY owned and finished rather than implicitly absent -
-- `sweeper` is 0 here because there is no bucket, and `unqueued[audit]` is 0
-- because every cell has a name, and those are two different facts.
work(w_effect_sweep, "DONE 2026-09-09: the effect layer's residue, swept in the same commit that declared the layer - 100 cells, 82 answered and 18 claimed by name, nothing left over").
work_state(w_effect_sweep, done).
work_sweeps(w_effect_sweep, effect).
-- ===========================================================================
-- 68. THE ES GLOBALS — the half of the standard library that is not a
-- prototype. Entered and closed 2026-09-09, in the wave the owner asked for
-- with the words `globals must be covered, without them all of this is
-- meaningless`.
--
-- WHAT `w_env_api_surface` LEFT, stated as the measurement that opened this
-- item rather than as an opinion: 130 members of eight PROTOTYPES, ten stdlib
-- calls in the corpus, ten attributed, zero unattributed — and NOT ONE static
-- of an ES global anywhere in the five call fixtures. `JSON.parse`,
-- `Promise.all`, `Object.entries` and `Math.max` were invisible to the era
-- question, and no audit could say so because the corpus had no site.
--
-- THE SAME SCANNER AND THE SAME COMPOSITION. scanners/ts_lib.ts grew a second
-- walk over the same `lib.es*.d.ts` files: 47 globals and 226 statics into
-- facts/js-globals.rofl, each dated by the earliest release that declares it,
-- by the `/// <reference lib=` graph that already dates the prototype half.
-- rules/js-globals.rofl joins it to the corpus. New relations, and the three
-- surfaces are three of them on purpose:
--   free_global[code](E, Name, File)      a reference nothing in the file binds
--   es_global[code](E, Name, Rel, Form)   ...and ECMAScript declares it
--   global_unattributed[audit](E, Name)   ...and it does not — the RUNTIME half
--   es_static[code](N, Name, Key, Rel)    `Object.entries`, dated
--   es_static_key[code]                   a well-known symbol is a KEY
--   es_static_call / es_global_invoke / es_global_construct
--   es_static_unattributed[audit]         a host extension of a language global
--   es_construct_not_constructor[audit]   `new Math()`, and WHY
--   es_global_unsupported / es_static_unsupported   the era gate, both surfaces
--
-- IT IS THE ES HALF OF THE EFFECT LAYER'S INPUT and that is why the split is
-- where it is. `layer(effect)` cannot derive `io` for `console.log` from the
-- SPELLING of `console`; `free_global[code]` says this node is a reference to a
-- name nothing binds, and `global_unattributed[audit]` is exactly the set a
-- runtime table joins against. Both are keyed by NODE and by NAME, so an effect
-- rule reaches `callee_of[code]` on one side and a runtime table on the other
-- without re-deriving anything.
--
-- THE CORPUS GREW BY ONE FILE AND NOT BY A LINE IN alpha.mjs.
-- test/fixtures/js-globals/globals.mjs.txt, thirteen sites each of which is the
-- only one of its kind, listed in its own header. It is `.txt` because
-- test/js-modules.test.ts imports every `.mjs` in test/fixtures/js-call and
-- compares default exports set-for-set, so a sixth module there is another
-- branch's oracle moving under it.
work(w_es_globals, "DONE 2026-09-09: the ES globals — binding, statics and the bridge to the prototype surface — generated from TypeScript`s lib.es*.d.ts and joined to a corpus").
work_state(w_es_globals, done).
work_spawned(w_es_globals, f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart).
work_spawned(w_es_globals, f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date).
work_spawned(w_es_globals, f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves).
work_spawned(w_es_globals, f_a_generated_pack_with_no_regeneration_gate_has_already_rotted).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 69. THE PROTOTYPE OF A CONSTRUCTED VALUE — the hole the globals bridge made
-- visible, entered 2026-09-09 by w_es_globals rather than found by reading.
--
-- `lib_global_prototype` bridges a global's NAME to the lower-case prototype
-- atom `lib_member` is keyed by, and it covers EIGHT of forty-seven because
-- eight is what `kind_prototype` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl can name a receiver
-- for. `Promise`, `Map`, `Set`, `WeakMap`, `Date` and every typed array are in
-- the other thirty-nine, so `holder.set(k, v)` after `const holder = new Map()`
-- reaches NEITHER half of the library: `kind_prototype` has no arm for a
-- `new_expression`, `prototype_of[flow]` is therefore empty, and
-- `stdlib_member[audit]` does not fire — measured on the fixture, which calls
-- `.set` and produces no residue row at all.
--
-- THE PIECES ARE STANDING AND THE DECISION IS NOT. `es_global_construct[code](X,
-- Name, Rel)` already names a construction of an ES global, so the arm is a
-- join from that to `lib_global_prototype` to `lib_member`. What it waits on is
-- whether `prototype_of[flow]` should carry a value whose class is not in this
-- program — the same question `w_prototype_of_a_value` answered for literals and
-- left unasked for constructions — and what that costs `may_be_node` and the
-- ambiguity audits.
work(w_prototype_of_a_constructed_value, "`new Map()` reaches no prototype, so a call on the instance is outside both halves of the library; the join exists and the decision does not").
work_state(w_prototype_of_a_constructed_value, open).
